{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>On the seventh day a second time.</b> This implies that the affliction is not declared clean unless it has dimmed, and so Rashi explained: 'dimmed' — that it has grown dimmer than its appearance; hence, if it stayed at its appearance or spread, it is impure. And this is a widespread halakha: if it stood as it was in the second week and did not spread, it is pure. And thus we learned in Masekhet Negaim: four appearances combine with each other for isolation, for exemption, and for definitive declaration — i.e., for isolation: that which stood during the first week; for exemption: that which stood at the end of the second week. And the same applies if it was white as snow at first and at the end of the week like the lime of the Temple — it is not considered dimmed but standing, until it diminishes from the appearances of affliction. And one may say that therefore it states here 'and behold, it has dimmed' — for if it had stood at exactly its appearance it would be pure; rather, it was stated for an added stringency, lest you say that if it changed to lime or from lime to snow, it is a new affliction and requires isolation as at first — therefore it teaches us that it is considered standing and is exempt. Another interpretation: therefore it states 'and behold, it has dimmed' — if it changed, even if it afterward spread, it is not declared impure. Therefore it is stated: 'if the affliction has dimmed and has not spread' — to say: the implication is that if it spread, it is impure even if it dimmed, for 'if it spread' that comes after it refers back to 'dimmed' stated above."], :book :leviticus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:08:06.071962463Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}